<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phocion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 402–318 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phocion was an Athenian statesman and general, elected general forty-five times, known for his austere honesty. He helped relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 340 BCE but generally urged accommodation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> fell under Macedonian control he led its government, and when democracy was briefly restored in 318 BCE he was condemned and executed by hemlock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phocion.jpg" alt="Phocion" width="320" height="615" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François-Nicolas Delaistre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phocion._Fran%C3%A7ois-Nicolas_Delaistre._Collection_Mus%C3%A9e_des_Beaux-Arts-Mairie_de_Bordeaux.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
